---
name: pr-reviewer
description: >
  Pre-merge multi-detector PR review for WordPress plugins (PHP + Vue + JSX + JS).
  Fans out 6 specialised detector passes (a11y, async-state, traceability, RBAC alignment,
  backward-compat, error-state) over a branch diff, aggregates findings, dedupes on
  (file, line, category), and writes an autharif-shaped report (per-line inline findings
  with bold lead-ins + summary review with severity tally). Re-runnable on the same branch
  to clear resolved findings.
when_to_use: >
  Before pushing a branch. After implementing a feature. When asked "review this PR",
  "give me a pre-merge gate", "run the orchestrator", "deep review", "find what autharif would catch".
context: fork
allowed-tools: Read Bash(git *) Bash(grep *) Bash(find *) Bash(rg *)
effort: high
---

# PR Reviewer (Multi-Detector Orchestrator)

This skill is the local pre-merge equivalent of the WPManageNinja AI-assisted reviewer
on GitHub. It catches the recurring categories that `engineering-review`'s monolithic
pass misses: accessibility, async state machine correctness, end-to-end UI↔backend
traceability inside one PR, RBAC alignment between UI gates and policy classes,
backward-compat regressions, and error-state UX.

If you only need the lightweight pre-merge sanity sweep, use `engineering-review`.
This skill is the heavy gate — six parallel detectors, slower, deeper.

## Live branch context

- **Diff stat:** !`git diff origin/dev...HEAD --stat 2>/dev/null || git diff HEAD~1...HEAD --stat`
- **Commits:** !`git log origin/dev...HEAD --oneline 2>/dev/null || git log HEAD~3..HEAD --oneline`
- **Changed files:** !`git diff origin/dev...HEAD --name-only 2>/dev/null || git diff HEAD~1..HEAD --name-only`

---

## Two modes

**First run** — no existing report at the output path: run all applicable detectors and write findings.

**Re-run** — report exists for this branch: read it, re-check every OPEN finding only,
mark resolved findings as `[cleared]`, archive the prior report as
`<branch>-<short-sha>.md` in the same directory, and report counts.

## Output path

Primary: `/Volumes/Workspace/pr-reviews/<repo>/<branch-slug>.md`

Fallback (when `/Volumes/Workspace` is not mounted): `<repo-root>/.review/<branch-slug>.md`

Choose the fallback automatically if the primary path's parent directory does not exist.
Do not create `/Volumes/Workspace/pr-reviews/` if it is missing — that signals the
machine is not configured for hosted reviews and the fallback is correct.

---

## Step 0a — Build the feedback loop FIRST

(Borrowed from matt-pocock's `diagnose` skill: no amount of detector-tuning helps
without a fast pass/fail signal.)

Before running detectors against a real branch for the first time after a criteria
pack edit, run the validation harness:

```bash
bash /Volumes/Projects/Tools/agent-skills/shared/scripts/audit-autharif.sh \
  --corpus /Volumes/Projects/Tools/agent-skills/pr-reviewer/references/test-corpus.tsv \
  --validate
```

This feeds the canonical 30-row corpus through the detector category map and
confirms each headline lands in the expected detector. ≥80% accuracy required to
proceed. If validation fails, the criteria packs are out of sync with reality —
fix them before invoking pr-reviewer on any real PR.

For routine pre-merge runs (criteria packs unchanged since last validation), skip
this step.

## Step 0b — Project glossary awareness

(Borrowed from matt-pocock's `grill-with-docs` skill.)

If a `CONTEXT.md` or `CONTEXT-MAP.md` exists at the repo root, read it once before
any detector fans out. Findings should use **project vocabulary** in headlines,
not generic best-practice labels. If the project glossary defines `PmproImport`
as the migration flow and the bug is in that flow, the headline says
"PmproImport retry resumes from stale offset" — not "async function lacks
re-entry guard."

This matters because reviewers reading both autharif's GitHub comments and
pr-reviewer's local report should not feel like they're reading two different
codebases. Autharif uses project terms (`PMPro`, `MCP`, `BlockComposer`,
`AiPolicy`) and pr-reviewer must too.

## Step 0c — Decide which detectors to run

Read the diff. Map changed-file extensions to detectors:

| Extension | Detectors that run |
|---|---|
| `.vue` | `a11y`, `async-state`, `error-state`, `traceability`, `bc-regression` |
| `.jsx` / `.tsx` | `a11y`, `async-state`, `error-state`, `traceability`, `bc-regression` |
| `.js` / `.ts` | `async-state`, `error-state`, `traceability`, `bc-regression` |
| `.php` (Controllers, Policies) | `rbac-alignment`, `traceability`, `bc-regression`, `perf-and-integrity` |
| `.php` (Views, Services, Models) | `traceability`, `bc-regression`, `perf-and-integrity` |
| `.scss` / `.css` | `a11y` (focus-visible / outline removal only) |
| `migrations/*` | `bc-regression`, `perf-and-integrity` |

The six detectors in this skill correspond to six reference packs in `references/`.
A seventh sub-pack `perf-and-integrity-criteria.md` covers performance and data
integrity — combined because the autharif corpus shows they co-occur on the same
files (controllers, services, migrations) at high rates.

Always run `traceability` and `bc-regression` regardless of file types — they are
PR-shape checks, not file-type checks.

---

## Step 1 — Fan out the detectors

If the runtime can spawn sub-agents (Claude Code Agent tool, Codex sub-agents),
spawn one sub-agent per detector in **parallel**. Each sub-agent receives:

```yaml
detector: <detector-name>             # one of: a11y, async-state, traceability, rbac-alignment, bc-regression, error-state, perf-and-integrity
diff_path: <abs path to git diff file or "use git">
changed_files: <list of file paths from the diff>
repo_root: <abs path to repo>
criteria_ref: references/<detector>-criteria.md
output_format: ndjson                 # one finding per line, schema below
max_findings: 50                      # cap per detector to prevent runaway
```

The sub-agent reads its criteria reference, walks every changed file relevant to
its category, and emits **NDJSON to stdout** — one finding per line. No prose,
no narrative — only findings.

If sub-agents are not available, emulate this as **six sequential passes** in this
skill's outer agent. Do not skip detectors when running sequentially; just run them
in series and concatenate the NDJSON.

---

## Step 2 — Canonical finding schema

Every finding from every detector MUST be a JSON object with exactly these fields:

```json
{
  "category": "a11y",
  "severity": "Blocking",
  "file": "resources/admin/Modules/Settings/Migration/Pmpro/_PmproProgress.vue",
  "line": 41,
  "headline": "Retry can start concurrent import loops",
  "evidence": "retryImport() called from button handler without checking if importInFlight === true",
  "impact": "Two concurrent import loops mutate state in parallel; progress UI double-counts and import can corrupt data.",
  "recommended_fix": "Guard retryImport() with `if (this.importInFlight) return;` and await prior loop's completion before resuming.",
  "detectors": ["async-state"]
}
```

**Required fields:** `category`, `severity`, `file`, `headline`, `evidence`, `impact`, `recommended_fix`, `detectors`.

**Optional:** `line` (omit for whole-file findings).

**Severity tiers:** `Blocking | High | Medium | Suggestion` — same vocabulary autharif uses.

**Category vocabulary** (closed set):
`a11y`, `async-state`, `traceability`, `rbac-alignment`, `bc-regression`, `error-state`,
`security`, `data-integrity`, `perf`, `ui-logic`.

A finding missing any required field MUST be dropped by the orchestrator with a
tagged stderr log (matt-pocock's `diagnose` convention — every log line gets a
unique prefix so cleanup is one `grep -v`):

| Tag | Used when |
|---|---|
| `[pr-reviewer:detector]` | Detector start / completion / count |
| `[pr-reviewer:drop]` | Finding rejected (malformed, over cap, out-of-vocabulary) |
| `[pr-reviewer:dedup]` | Two findings merged on `(file, line, category)` |
| `[pr-reviewer:precedence]` | Cross-category collision resolved by precedence list |
| `[pr-reviewer:cleared]` | Re-run mode: prior finding no longer reproduces |

Example:
```
[pr-reviewer:drop] async-state finding missing 'severity' field
[pr-reviewer:dedup] resources/.../_PmproProgress.vue:41 (async-state) merged 2 findings
[pr-reviewer:precedence] AllProducts.vue:174 — kept rbac-alignment, dropped a11y
```

Tail the orchestrator with `2>&1 | grep '\[pr-reviewer:'` to audit decisions
post-run.

---

## Step 3 — Aggregate and dedup

Collect all NDJSON output from all detectors. For each finding:

1. **Dedup key:** `(file, line, category)`. Two findings with the same key are merged.
2. **Cross-category collision:** when two detectors flag the same `(file, line)` with
   different categories, keep one finding using this fixed precedence:

   ```
   security > rbac-alignment > data-integrity > traceability > async-state > a11y > bc-regression > error-state > ui-logic > perf
   ```

3. **Merge rules:**
   - `category` ← higher-precedence finding's category
   - `severity` ← max of both severities
   - `headline` ← higher-severity finding's headline
   - `detectors` ← union of both `detectors` arrays
   - `evidence` / `impact` / `recommended_fix` ← higher-precedence finding's text

4. Sort findings by `severity` (Blocking → Suggestion), then by `category` precedence,
   then by `file` alphabetic, then by `line` ascending.

---

## Step 4 — Render the report

Output shape mirrors autharif's GitHub review comments. Two top-level sections:

### Section A — Summary

```markdown
## Summary

**Blocking:** 4 | **High:** 7 | **Medium:** 12 | **Suggestion:** 3

### Blocking
- `app/Http/Controllers/Migration/Pmpro/PmproMigrationController.php:92` — **PMPro routes are wired to nonfunctional handlers** (traceability)
- `resources/admin/Modules/Settings/Migration/Pmpro/_PmproProgress.vue:41` — **Retry can start concurrent import loops** (async-state)
- ...

### High
- ...
```

For each tier, list one bullet per finding with `<file>:<line>` and `**headline**` and `(category)`.

### Section B — Inline findings

```markdown
## Inline findings

### `resources/admin/Modules/Settings/Migration/Pmpro/_PmproProgress.vue:41` — async-state — Blocking

**Retry can start concurrent import loops**

**Evidence:** retryImport() called from button handler without checking if importInFlight === true.

**Impact:** Two concurrent import loops mutate state in parallel; progress UI double-counts and import can corrupt data.

**Recommended fix:** Guard retryImport() with `if (this.importInFlight) return;` and await prior loop's completion before resuming.

**Detectors:** `async-state`

---
```

One block per finding, separated by `---`. The first line bold-headlined matches
the autharif `<strong>...</strong>` lead-in convention so the two reports read the
same way.

---

## Step 5 — Re-run mode (when report exists)

If the output path already has a report:

1. Read existing findings from `## Inline findings`.
2. Re-run the detectors. For each new finding, check if a prior finding has the
   same `(file, line, headline)`:
   - **Match:** keep it (still open).
   - **Prior exists, no match in new:** mark `[cleared]` in the new report — the
     issue is fixed.
   - **New, no prior match:** add as a fresh finding.
3. Get the current short sha: `git rev-parse --short HEAD`.
4. Move the existing report to `<branch>-<short-sha>.md`.
5. Write the new report.
6. Append a `## Re-run history` section listing each run with sha, timestamp,
   and counts (mirrors autharif's "Rechecked [`abc1234`]. Some blocking findings were cleared." convention).

---

## Step 6 — Pre-flight announcement (do this before Step 1)

(Borrowed from `diagnose` Phase 3: announce the plan before executing — gives the
user a cheap interrupt point.)

Before fanning out detectors, print a one-line plan:

```
[pr-reviewer:plan] 14 changed files (8 vue, 4 php, 2 scss).
                   Detectors: a11y, async-state, error-state, traceability, bc-regression, rbac-alignment, perf-and-integrity.
                   Skipping: (none).
                   Estimated time: ~2 min parallel / ~8 min sequential.
```

If the user has reason to skip a detector ("we know this is a CSS-only PR, skip
async-state"), they can interrupt and re-invoke with `--skip async-state`.

---

## Required behaviors (do not skip)

1. **Always run `traceability` and `bc-regression`** — they are PR-shape checks, not
   file-type checks. A PHP-only PR can still introduce a payload-key rename.
2. **Never report a finding outside the closed category vocabulary** — if a real issue
   doesn't fit a detector, add it to the most-relevant detector's criteria reference
   and re-run; do not invent a new category at runtime.
3. **Severity is conservative by default** — anything ambiguous is `Suggestion`, not
   `Blocking`. Promote to `Blocking` only when the criteria reference says so.
4. **Cap detector output at 50 findings** — runaway detectors hurt readability more
   than missing edge cases.
5. **Always emit the canonical schema** — even prose-style findings get reduced to
   the JSON shape before rendering. The orchestrator does not render free-form text.

## Anti-patterns (do not do)

- Do **not** fold `pr-reviewer` into `engineering-review`. They serve different
  cost/depth points; both should remain available.
- Do **not** add a seventh detector before exhausting the existing six. New finding
  categories belong in an existing detector's criteria reference, not as a new
  parallel pass.
- Do **not** report findings in `# Severity` section style if you skipped the inline
  block — every summary entry must have a corresponding inline block.
- Do **not** silently drop findings on dedup — always log to stderr what was merged.
